.TH JANUS 1 "2026-11-19" "Janus Confidential CLI development build" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
janus \- manage SSH devices with Keychain credentials and reusable sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B janus
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Janus Confidential CLI saves SSH device metadata locally and passwords only in macOS Keychain.
An authenticated OpenSSH master supplies independent terminal sessions.
SSH operations require macOS, system OpenSSH and the installed native helper.
Help, version and completion do not require a device or credential.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B devices [--list | --json]
List saved devices; table output is the default. Columns are ID, NAME, PLATFORM,
TYPE, CONNECTED and READY. CONNECTED means a master accepts new sessions.
A connected device is ready. READY means TCP reachability, not authentication.
Unknown is a dash (JSON null); success is green, failure red on a colour terminal.
Listing performs bounded network probes but never opens a credential.
.TP
.B profile [ID|NAME] [--json] [--refresh] [--database NAME] [--table NAME]
Read cached device observations without connecting. Refresh uses an authorized
connection and native approval when required. MySQL database/table selection
collects structure only, never business rows. Failed refresh retains old data.
.TP
.B spawn USER@HOST [-p PORT]
Register a device or connect to its existing profile. Port defaults to 22.
Passwords are entered in native UI and saved to Keychain. SSH arguments are passed directly; Janus Confidential CLI reserves master control (-M, -S, -O).
Failed login leaves the profile saved for retry.
.TP
.B spawn mysql USER@HOST [-p 3306] [--database NAME] [--ca FILE]
Verify TLS and account authentication using native Keychain approval, then register
and open an interactive SQL session. End SQL with a semicolon; use \eq to exit.
.TP
.B credential [--json]
List saved account metadata. Account or device IDs/names are accepted for recovery. --edit or --recover ID|NAME verifies a replacement
password before selecting it and revoking old SQL sessions. --remove ID|NAME
removes an unreferenced account and its selected Keychain item.
.TP
.B session list|close|revoke [SESSION_ID]
List managed MySQL sessions as JSON, or terminate a specified session. These
commands do not enumerate existing SSH or A2X sessions.
.TP
.B spawn agent USER@HOST [-p 7263] [--agent-transport subsystem|stdio] [--share-mcp FILE]
Register an AI agent device and create a new Agenux session. Default transport:
SSH subsystem agenux. The explicit stdio option uses a fixed launcher: find
agenux in PATH, otherwise use $HOME/.local/bin/agenux, always with argument stdio.
.TP
.B connect [ID|NAME] [--empty] [--share-mcp FILE]
Choose the connection adapter from the saved device platform and type.
Without a device, show all devices; --empty restricts that selector to AI agents.
SSH devices open a terminal. Agents resume a single available session, always
select among multiple sessions (including a new-session option), or create a
session when none exists. The last session only determines initial picker focus.
--empty always creates a new agent session. Real terminals enter the native Pi
REPL over A2X PTY with raw bytes and resize forwarding; local terminal state is
restored on exit. TERM, COLORTERM and NO_COLOR are forwarded. Old servers must
be upgraded for native UI. Non-TTY input uses one task.send message per line;
/exit closes the client. Server-required MFA uses a native six-digit verification dialog
before session selection; verification codes are not logged or persisted. A reusable master avoids another authentication request.
--share-mcp explicitly shares the exact tool names in the supplied local JSON
configuration for this connection only. Without it no local tools are shared.
.TP
.B bridge [ID|NAME]
Bridge desktop clients to authenticated A2X/Pi sessions using newline-delimited
JSON on stdin/stdout. Without a target, list saved agent devices as JSON.
Reuse existing SSH and native authentication; one device per process.
.TP
.B credential --recover ID|NAME
Accept an account or device ID/name. MySQL devices resolve to their linked account.
For SSH and Agent devices, enroll a separate SSH credential using native UI. Verify it using a
separate SSH master before switching the saved credential reference. Preserve
the old Keychain item and active sessions. Failed attempts do not change the
selection; an unselected staged Keychain item may remain. Recovered records
require Janus 0.1.6 or later.
.TP
.B disconnect [ID|NAME ...]
Stop master reuse without interrupting existing sessions. The old transport exits
when its existing channels close. Without devices, show the multi-select picker.
Unmatched devices or failures do not stop the batch.
.TP
.B remove [ID|NAME ...]
Accept account or device IDs/names. Accounts must be unreferenced; their selected
Keychain credential is deleted. Ambiguous names require a full ID.
For devices, delete the profile and its exact SSH Keychain credential. MySQL accounts remain;
use credential --remove after removing all device references. Active connections continue.
If another saved device uses the same SSH account, host and port, retain the
shared credential until the last such device is removed.
If credential deletion fails, retain the device for retry.
Without arguments, select devices or accounts in the multi-select picker.
Both remove and disconnect deduplicate matching devices, continue after errors,
and print totals for success, failure, unmatched arguments and skipped duplicates.
Summary numbers are highlighted on terminals unless NO_COLOR is set.
Any failure or unmatched argument yields a nonzero exit status after completion.
.TP
.B rename ID|NAME NEW_NAME
Rename the device. Names must be unique, case-insensitively. Quote names with spaces.
.TP
.B help [COMMAND]
Show help. Command-specific --help is also supported.
.TP
.B version | --version
Print exactly two lines: build version/date and repository URL.
.TP
.B completion bash|zsh|fish
Generate completion for the chosen shell. Device candidates use read-only metadata.
.TP
.B upgrade [--yes]
Check the latest stable GitHub release. Ask before updating unless --yes is given.
Empty input, EOF, no and invalid responses cancel. Non-interactive input without
--yes fails if an update is available. Source builds require a manual rebuild.
Homebrew installs remain managed by their owning Formula. Never implicitly downgrade.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
janus spawn builder@example.com -p 3233
janus devices --json
janus rename builder@example.com 'Build Server'
janus connect 'Build Server'
janus disconnect 'Build Server'
janus remove 'Build Server'
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.janus/base/devices.json
Private, versioned device metadata; contains no passwords.
.TP
.B ~/.janus/base/profiles/
Private device observations with identity bindings and freshness timestamps.
.TP
.B ~/.janus/base/ssh/
Private OpenSSH control sockets. Device UUIDs remain stable across renames.
.TP
.B macOS login Keychain
SSH password service: app.janus.confidential.cli.ssh.password.v1:HOST:PORT; account: SSH username.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable coloured status marks.
.TP
.B JANUS_TRACE=1
Write phase timings to stderr without password data. A submitted system
authentication request does not establish when its window became visible.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero means success or a cancelled upgrade; nonzero means invalid input or failure.
A closed interactive SSH session propagates failure as a nonzero Janus Confidential CLI exit.
.SH CAVEATS
Disconnect stops new reuse; old sessions do not imply CONNECTED is true.
Remove cannot revoke credentials already in use by an established SSH connection.
Performance targets require real-device verification; no latency guarantee is claimed.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh_config (5),
.BR security (1)
